  "query_preview": "..\n(Thursday Night through Wednesday)\nIssued at 1032 PM CDT Wed Oct 21 2026\n\nA strong upper level high will remain overhead for the remainder of \nthe week and into the weekend. Meanwhile, a robust upper level low \nacross the southwestern USA will only make minor progress moving \neast. The net effect will be continued well above normal \ntemperatures with dry conditions. \n\nAs the upper level low nudges closer to the area Friday into this \nweekend we will see increased southerly wind gusts of 15-25 mph. \nThis may locally increase fire weather concerns, especially \nnorthwest of I-44, where winds will be strongest. \n\nBy early next week the upper level low will eject to the north of \nthe forecast area, but will pass close enough to push the area of \nhigh pressure to the east. This will open up the door to stronger \nflow aloft along with periodic transient shortwaves. There is not \ncurrently any signal for significant precipitation, but the \nprobabilities do appear to be increasing for at least some rainfall \nto occur. Temperatures will will likely decrease closer to normal \ndue to the increased cloud cover and rain chances as well as the \npotential for minor cool air intrusions from weak frontal systems.",
